title: Molecular Orbitals
manual: fields/

Displays the molecular orbitals of the reference field of the system as isosurfaces, with
an energy-level diagram. The reference field must be a molecular wavefunction (wfn, wfx,
fchk, molden, ...). Select an orbital in the table or in the diagram and its positive and
negative lobes are drawn in the view as a transient isosurface pair; select it again to hide
it. The orbital grids are cached, so an orbital already shown comes back at once.

# Options

- "Reference field" and "Wavefunction": the field and a summary of its orbitals (occupied and
  virtual). "Reload with Virtual Orbitals" re-reads the wavefunction file including the
  unoccupied orbitals, when the format contains them (fchk and molden).
- "Orbital": "Energy units" (Hartree or eV) for the table and the diagram, and "Diagram"
  shows the energy-level diagram beside the orbital table. The table lists each orbital with
  its id, a dot if its grid is cached, its label (HOMO, LUMO-1, ...), spin, occupation and
  energy. "Frontier" scrolls back to the HOMO/LUMO boundary. In the diagram, click a level to
  select it, or a pile of merged levels to zoom into it; "Frontier" and "All" set its range.
- "Display": "Quality" is the density of the grid of the isosurface; automatic uses the
  default, coarsened if sampling one orbital would take too long, and a slow choice is
  flagged. "Isovalue (a.u.)" of the positive/negative pair, "Opacity" and the colors of the
  "Positive lobe" and "Negative lobe".
- "Create Isosurface Object": copies the displayed orbital into a permanent isosurface
  object, editable from the Objects menu of the view.

# Notes

The orbital isosurface is transient: it disappears when the window closes unless it was made
into an object. The orbitals can also be used in the console and in arithmetic expressions
through the field modifiers of the fields page (for instance "$1:4" is orbital 4 of field 1,
and labels such as HOMO and LUMO are also available).
